{"about":"20b35eec8f966227f76f4aa3b7bd3ff43fbc8f61e67d869cfe6502d6db030ca0","body":"The conjecture falsely equates the rejection of inductive probability with the absence of any rational criterion for action. Popper explicitly distinguishes between 'probability' (logical likelihood of truth) and 'corroboration' (a report on past performance under severe testing). The rational basis for preference is not that the better-corroborated theory is more likely to be true, but that it has demonstrated greater explanatory power and resilience against falsification attempts. Choosing it is a methodological rule based on the principle of learning from errors, not an inductive inference that the future will resemble the past.\n[would settle: A definitive textual analysis of Popper's distinction between 'acceptance' for practical purposes versus 'belief' in truth, specifically regarding his concept of verisimilitude (truth-likeness) as a non-probabilistic guide for preference.]","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}